  "conclusion": {
    "q1_qlike_ceiling": "GJR-GARCH QLIKE ceiling does NOT hold for commodities. GARCH(1,1) wins USO; GJR marginally wins GLD (DM p=0.068, not significant). Symmetric GARCH is sufficient.",
    "q2_leverage_direction": "GLD: standard negative gamma (-0.029, 46% significant, weak). USO: INVERTED positive gamma (+0.078, 100% significant, strong). Oil price UP = vol UP, consistent with supply shock dynamics.",
    "q3_har_log_range": "HAR log-range FAILS for both commodities. Excluded from MCS. GLD: HAR QLIKE 2.82 vs GJR 1.48 (-90% worse). USO: HAR 1.92 vs GARCH 1.36 (-38% worse). Range-based models do not work for commodities.",
    "q4_semivariance": "Semivariance works for USO (MCS included, p=0.18) but not better than GARCH. For GLD: Semivar also in MCS (p=0.49) but gamma\u22480 confirms K453 finding.",
    "q5_mcs_superior_set": "GLD: 5-model set (EWMA/GARCH/Semivar/EGARCH/GJR). USO: 6-model set (all except HAR). Key: GARCH(1,1) has p=1.0 in both = hardest to beat in commodities.",
    "novel_finding": "Commodities are the OPPOSITE of equities for vol forecasting: (1) Symmetric GARCH beats asymmetric models, (2) HAR is worst not best, (3) Leverage effect is inverted for oil. This extends Paper 1 cross-asset results to a new asset class."
